const raagDetails = {
  "Yaman": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma(t) Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa — Re Ga — Ma(t) Ga Re Sa — Ni(lower) Re Ga Ma(t) Dha — Ni Sa", narrative: "Yaman, also called Kalyan, is the most beloved of all evening raags and perhaps the most widely taught raag in Hindustani music. Its defining feature is the tivra (sharp) Madhyam, which gives the raag an open, luminous, reaching quality — as though the music is straining upward toward something just beyond grasp. Performed at the onset of night, Yaman embodies the ras of Shringar in its most refined, aesthetic sense: not the heat of longing but the beauty of the moment itself, the soft transition from day to dark. Its vadi Gandhar is approached with slow, deliberate meend, and the characteristic movement through Ni–Sa and Ma(t)–Pa gives it an effortless expansiveness that makes it a natural canvas for both long alap and ornate compositions. <em>Variance: The Gwalior gharana traditionally begins the arohana from Ni in the lower octave (Ni Re Ga Ma(t)…) rather than from Sa; the Kirana and Jaipur gharanas tend to begin from Sa directly. Some older treatises and the Agra gharana treat the vadi as Ga while others assign it to Ni — the Ga vadi is now the accepted standard in most traditions.</em>" },
  "Yaman Kalyan": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Ma(t) Pa — Pa Ma Ga — Ma Re Sa — Ni(lower) Re Ga Ma(t) Dha Ni Sa", narrative: "Yaman Kalyan is a blend of Yaman and Kalyan that employs both the shuddha and tivra Madhyam, giving it a quality somewhat softer and more introspective than pure Yaman. The shuddha Ma appears in descent, lending the raag a grounding tenderness that Yaman alone does not possess. This dual Ma usage creates moments of beautiful harmonic ambiguity, and the overall character sits between the luminous openness of Yaman and a quieter, more meditative resolve. It is favored in slower khayal where the interchange of the two Madhyams can be explored with nuance and deliberateness." },
  "Kedar": { swaras: "Sa Ma Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Ma", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Ma Pa Ma(t) Pa Dha Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma(t) Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Ma Pa — Ma(t) Pa Dha Pa — Ni Dha Pa Ma — Ma Ga Re Sa", narrative: "Kedar is an evening raag of devotional sweetness, rooted in the Kalyan thaat through its use of tivra Madhyam, yet with a character distinctly its own. Its arohana is vakra — it zigzags, omits Re and Ga in ascent — and this indirect approach gives the raag a searching, circling quality, as though devotion expressed not in a straight declaration but in a yearning orbit around the divine. The Madhyam is the vadi, and the raag dwells there with great feeling, approached from Pa in characteristic looping phrases. Kedar is closely associated with bhakti compositions and carries an almost temple-like gravity beneath its sweetness." },
  "Hameer": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Dha", samvadi: "Ga", arohana: "Sa Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga Ma(t) Pa — Dha Ni Dha Pa — Ma Ga Re Sa — Ni(lower) Re Ga Ma(t) Dha", narrative: "Hameer is a raag of grandeur and majesty, performed in the first quarter of the night. It uses both Madhyams — the tivra in ascent and shuddha in descent — and its vadi Dhaivata sits in the upper part of the octave, giving the raag an elevated, expansive quality. The arohana skips Re and Ma, moving in broad leaps that convey strength and confidence. Hameer is said to embody a kind of heroic romance — the Shringar and Veer ras mingled — and it has been the vehicle for some of the grandest khayal compositions in the tradition, particularly in the Gwalior and Agra gharanas." },
  "Kamod": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Pa", samvadi: "Re", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Pa Dha Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Pa — Dha Pa Ma Ga — Pa Ma Ga Re Sa — Re Ga Pa Dha Sa", narrative: "Kamod is a lively, festive raag of the night, belonging to the Kalyan thaat but with a character that leans joyful and celebratory rather than meditative. Its vakra movements — particularly the characteristic Pa–Ma–Ga–Pa oscillation in descent — give it a playful, folk-tinged personality. The vadi Pancham is strong and repeated, and the raag has a buoyancy that makes it a natural choice for thumri and lighter khayal. Despite its simplicity of mood, Kamod requires careful attention to its specific phrase shapes — without them, it easily collapses into Yaman or Bhoopali." },
  "Nand": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Ma Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Ma(t) Pa — Ni Dha Pa — Ma Ga Ma Re Sa — Ni(lower) Re Ga Ma", narrative: "Nand, also known as Anandi, is a night raag of the Kalyan thaat that carries an expansive, romantic quality. It employs both Madhyams with the tivra Ma prominent in ascent and shuddha Ma in the characteristic vakra descent. The raag has a flowing, unhurried quality — its phrases tend to spread across both octaves with ease — and its emotional world is one of contented longing rather than acute yearning. Nand is particularly beloved in the Jaipur and Gwalior gharanas, where its breadth is explored through long, slow alap before the rhythmic compositions begin." },
  "Shuddha Kalyan": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma(t) Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga — Ma(t) Ga Re Sa — Ni(lower) Re Ga Ma(t) Pa — Dha Ni Sa", narrative: "Shuddha Kalyan uses only the tivra Madhyam throughout — both in ascent and descent — distinguishing it from Yaman Kalyan which mixes both Ma variants. This consistency gives the raag a pure, sattvic character: bright without being restless, devotional without heaviness. It is considered a purer, more orthodox form of the Kalyan family and is often taught to students before Yaman precisely because its phrases are clean and its rules unambiguous. The mood is serene and uplifting, befitting the early evening performance time." },
  "Bhoopali": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Pa Dha", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Dha", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Pa Dha Sa", avarohana: "Sa Dha Pa Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga — Pa Ga Re Sa — Dha Pa Ga — Re Ga Pa Dha Sa", narrative: "Bhoopali is a pentatonic raag of striking simplicity and beauty, using only five swaras with no Madhyam or Nishad. This austerity of material paradoxically gives the raag enormous expressive power — there is nowhere to hide, and every phrase must carry full emotional weight. The vadi Gandhar is the emotional center, and the characteristic movement between Ga–Pa and the cadential Re–Sa gives Bhoopali its quality of open, melancholic warmth. Performed at dusk, it is one of the first raags most students learn, yet its deceptive simplicity means that true mastery — finding depth within five notes — takes a lifetime." },
  "Deshkar": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Pa Dha", vadi: "Dha", samvadi: "Ga", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Pa Dha Sa", avarohana: "Sa Dha Pa Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Pa Dha — Sa Dha Pa — Ga Pa Dha Sa — Re Ga Pa", narrative: "Deshkar shares the same five swaras as Bhoopali but differs crucially in its vadi (Dhaivata rather than Gandhar) and in the weight and treatment of phrases. Where Bhoopali lingers on Ga with feeling, Deshkar emphasizes Dha and approaches phrases with a brighter, more direct energy. Performed in the morning, its character is fresh and clear rather than melancholic — a morning light to Bhoopali's dusk glow. The distinction between these two pentatonic raags is a classic test of a musician's ear and understanding of how the same materials can yield entirely different emotional worlds." },
  "Hindol": { swaras: "Ga Ma(t) Dha Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Ga Ma(t) Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Ma(t) Ga Sa", aalap: "Ga Ma(t) Dha — Ni Dha Ma(t) Ga — Sa Ga Ma(t) Dha Ni Sa", narrative: "Hindol is a remarkable pentatonic spring raag that omits both Sa and Pa, using only Ga, Ma(t), Dha, and Ni as its core swaras (with Sa as a touch note in some phrases). This omission of the two most stable swaras gives Hindol a floating, ungrounded quality — it swings and sways rather than standing firm, which is why it is associated with the imagery of a jhula (swing) and the spring season. Its mood is joyful and festive, invoking the celebration of Holi and the exuberance of new growth. The tivra Madhyam glows at the heart of every phrase." },
  "Gorakh Kalyan": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma(t) Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga Ma(t) Pa — Ni Dha Pa Ma(t) — Ga Re Sa — Ni(lower) Sa Re Ga", narrative: "Gorakh Kalyan is an evening raag invoking the saint Gorakh Nath, carrying devotional weight within the Kalyan thaat. Its scale is similar to Yaman but its phrase vocabulary is distinctly different — more austere and contemplative, less romantically expansive. The raag is rare in mainstream concert performance but holds an important place in the dhrupad tradition, where its ascetic devotional quality finds its most natural expression." },
  "Bhoop Kalyan": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Pa Dha", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Dha", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Pa Dha Sa", avarohana: "Sa Dha Pa Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Pa — Dha Pa Ga Re Sa — Ga Pa Dha Sa", narrative: "Bhoop Kalyan is a pentatonic evening raag that blends elements of Bhoopali and Kalyan. Using the same five swaras as Bhoopali but with Kalyan-inflected phrase movements, it sits in the early evening slot with a bright, warm character. The raag is relatively rare and is distinguished from Bhoopali through specific phrase shapes and the emphasis of Dha in its movements, hinting at the Kalyan ancestry." },
  "Bilawal": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Dha", samvadi: "Ga", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa — Dha Pa Ma Ga — Ni Dha Pa — Ma Ga Re Sa", narrative: "Bilawal is the thaat raag corresponding to what Western music calls the major scale — all shuddha swaras, no alterations. Yet in Hindustani music it carries not the simple brightness that might be expected but a quality of pure sattvic clarity, performed in the morning when the mind is fresh and undistracted. The vadi Dhaivata sits high, and the raag's characteristic phrases move gracefully across both octaves. Bilawal is considered deeply auspicious and is often used in morning prayers and devotional compositions." },
  "Alhaiya Bilawal": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Dha", samvadi: "Ga", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha — Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga — Re Sa Ni(lower) Re Ga", narrative: "Alhaiya Bilawal is the most commonly performed raag of the Bilawal thaat, heard far more often in concerts than pure Bilawal itself. It shares the same swaras but has its own phrase vocabulary, characteristic movements, and a more celebratory mood. The raag takes its name from the legendary warrior Alha, and its character has a brightness and festive confidence that suits morning concert performances. Key phrases involve a distinctive treatment of Ni–Sa and Pa–Dha–Ni movements that distinguish it clearly from its parent thaat." },
  "Bihag": { swaras: "Sa Ga Ma Ma(t) Pa Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Ga Ma Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Pa Ma(t) Pa Ma Ga Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga Ma Pa — Ma(t) Pa Ni Sa — Ni Pa Ma(t) Ma Ga — Sa", narrative: "Bihag is a night raag of intimate romance, using both Madhyams with shuddha Ma in ascent and tivra Ma appearing in the characteristic vakra descent phrase Ma(t)–Pa–Ma–Ga. Re and Dha are largely absent, giving the raag a concentrated, interior quality. The vadi Gandhar is lingered upon with warmth, and the overall character is one of late-night tenderness rather than aching longing. Bihag is enormously popular in lighter classical and film music and is considered approachable and immediately beautiful, which has made it one of the most performed night raags in the modern repertoire." },
  "Hansadhwani": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Pa Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Pa Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Pa — Ni Pa Ga Re Sa — Ga Pa Ni Sa — Ni Pa Ga", narrative: "Hansadhwani is a pentatonic raag of pure joy and celebration, using five swaras with neither Madhyam nor Dhaivata. Originally a Carnatic raag (where it is extremely popular), it has been fully absorbed into Hindustani practice. Its five notes — Sa Re Ga Pa Ni — move with a lightness and inevitability that feels almost self-evidently beautiful, and it is among the most immediately pleasing raags in either tradition. The vadi Gandhar and samvadi Nishad create a pair of swaras that pull the music forward with irresistible momentum. Hansadhwani is often performed in evening concerts to uplift the mood." },
  "Nat Bilawal": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa — Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", narrative: "Nat Bilawal blends Nat and Bilawal, inheriting from Nat a certain strength and directness that sits beneath the brightness of Bilawal. The raag has a more energetic, assertive quality than pure Bilawal, and its phrases have the characteristic Nat movement of bold, direct ascent. It is an evening raag with heroic Shringar — confidence rather than sweetness — and is performed less frequently than Alhaiya Bilawal, appearing mainly in the Gwalior and Agra gharana repertoires." },
  "Durga": { swaras: "Sa Re Ma Pa Dha", vadi: "Re", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Dha Sa", avarohana: "Sa Dha Pa Ma Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa — Dha Pa Ma Re Sa — Ma Pa Dha Sa — Re Sa", narrative: "Durga is a pentatonic night raag of remarkable purity, omitting Ga and Ni entirely. This unusual choice of swaras gives Durga a wide-spaced, austere beauty — the intervals between notes are larger than most raags, and the music moves in expansive steps. The vadi Re and samvadi Pa create a tonal pull across the fifth, and the raag has a devotional, Goddess-invoking quality (its name directly references Durga, the divine feminine). Performed at night, Durga carries a calm, powerful serenity — not soft but still, like a deep body of water." },
  "Shankara": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Pa Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Pa Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga Pa Ni Sa — Ni Pa Ga Re Sa — Ga Pa Ni Sa", narrative: "Shankara is a pentatonic night raag invoking Lord Shiva, using the same five swaras as Hansadhwani but with an entirely different emotional world. Where Hansadhwani is bright and joyful, Shankara is austere and devotional, its phrases carrying weight and gravity appropriate to the deity it evokes. The absence of Madhyam and Dhaivata strips the raag down to its essence, and the characteristic movements — particularly the strong Ni–Sa and the deliberate treatment of Ga — distinguish it from its pentatonic cousin. It is a raag more heard in dhrupad than khayal." },
  "Devgiri Bilawal": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Dha", samvadi: "Ga", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Pa Ma Ga — Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa — Ni Dha Pa", narrative: "Devgiri Bilawal is a morning raag of serene, pastoral beauty belonging to the Bilawal family. Its character is fresh and contemplative, evoking the stillness of early morning in a natural setting. It is a relatively rare raag in concert performance but valued in the tradition for its pure, sattvic quality. The phrase movements follow a gentle, unhurried trajectory that makes it well-suited to slow alap before compositions." },
  "Saraswati": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga — Ma Pa Dha — Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", narrative: "Saraswati raag is a morning raag invoking the goddess of learning and music, fitting for the early hours when the mind is clear and receptive. Its character is pure and scholarly — not emotionally turbulent but quietly luminous, appropriate for a raag dedicated to the white-robed deity of wisdom. It is performed on the occasion of Vasant Panchami and other auspicious moments when Saraswati is worshipped, and its phrases carry a meditative, devotional weight." },
  "Charukeshi": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Pa", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa — Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", narrative: "Charukeshi is a Carnatic raag that has found a firm foothold in Hindustani music, and its unusual scale — combining the bright Re and Ga of Bilawal with the komal Dha and Ni of Bhairavi — gives it a strikingly beautiful, bittersweet character. The combination creates a raag that feels simultaneously open and shadowed, joyful and touched with pathos. Its vadi Pancham anchors the music in the middle register, and the characteristic descent through komal Dha and Ni adds a quality of gentle melancholy to what would otherwise be a bright raag. Charukeshi is beloved for its unique sound and relative independence from strict performance-time conventions." },
  "Khamaj": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga Ma Pa — Dha Pa Ma Ga — Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", narrative: "Khamaj is the parent thaat raag using komal Ni in descent and shuddha Ni in ascent, giving it a naturally folk-tinged, sensuous quality. It is one of the most important raags for thumri, dadra, and light classical forms, where its relaxed approach to swara usage — allowing considerable freedom with which swaras appear and how — makes it a supple vehicle for emotional expression. The raag has deep roots in folk and devotional music and carries an earthy romanticism that more formal raags lack. Its intimate, slightly informal character has made it a gateway raag for learning light classical forms." },
  "Des": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k)", vadi: "Pa", samvadi: "Re", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa — Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma — Ga Re Sa — Pa Ni(k) Sa", narrative: "Des is one of the most beloved raags in Hindustani music, carrying a quality that strikes most Indian listeners as deeply, intrinsically familiar — it sounds like home. Associated with the monsoon and performed at night, its characteristic vakra arohana (skipping Ga and Dha in ascent) gives it a forward-moving, yearning quality. The raag occupies an unusual emotional middle ground: romantic without being heavy, wistful without being mournful, patriotic without being assertive. Numerous famous film songs have been based on Des, cementing its place in the collective musical memory." },
  "Tilak Kamod": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Ni(k)", vadi: "Pa", samvadi: "Re", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Pa — Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga Re Sa — Ga Pa Ni(k) Sa", narrative: "Tilak Kamod is a sweet, romantic night raag with folk origins, closely associated with devotional compositions praising Lord Krishna. Its absence of Dha gives it a somewhat spare, direct quality, and the characteristic Pa–Ni–Sa movement in ascent carries a brightness and forward momentum. The raag is most at home in thumri and bhajan, where its simplicity and approachability allow for direct emotional expression. It is rarely treated to extended alap in formal khayal performance." },
  "Jhinjhoti": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa — Dha Ni(k) Dha Pa — Ma Ga Re Sa — Re Ga Ma Pa Dha", narrative: "Jhinjhoti is a night raag of accessible, bittersweet romance — one of the most commonly heard raags in thumri and light khayal. Its komal Ni in descent gives it that characteristic gentle sighing quality, while the vadi Gandhar carries the emotional weight of the raag. The boundary with Khamaj requires careful navigation through specific phrase choices, and the distinction lies primarily in how Ga is approached and emphasized. Jhinjhoti has been the vehicle for many of the most famous thumri compositions, and its warmth and folk accessibility have made it a favorite beyond classical music circles." },
  "Rageshri": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Ma — Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga — Re Sa Ni(lower) Re Ga", narrative: "Rageshri is a tender, intimate late-night raag that omits Dha, creating a concentrated emotional world. The absence of Dhaivata removes a swara that often provides stability and breadth, leaving the raag more inward and vulnerable-feeling. The characteristic Ga–Ma and Ni–Pa–Ma–Ga movements create a sense of loving introspection, and the raag is particularly associated with compositions expressing gentle longing between lovers. Its mood is quieter and more private than Khamaj, making it appropriate for the late hours when emotional life turns interior." },
  "Pahadi": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Pa Dha", vadi: "Sa", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Pa Dha Sa", avarohana: "Sa Dha Pa Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Pa — Dha Pa Ga Re Sa — Pa Ga Re Sa — Dha Sa", narrative: "Pahadi is a folk raag of mountain freshness and open skies, using five core swaras in a way that evokes the north Indian hills and their musical traditions. Its rules are relatively relaxed by classical standards — other swaras may appear in ornamentation — and this flexibility gives Pahadi a free, improvisatory character that sets it apart from more rigidly defined raags. The raag's pentatonic simplicity belies its emotional range: it can be playful, wistful, or deeply lyrical depending on the performer, and it has been extraordinarily influential in Hindi film music." },
  "Mand": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k)", vadi: "Sa", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa — Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa — Ni(k) Sa", narrative: "Mand is a folk raag from the Rajasthani tradition, associated with the desert celebrations of that region. Its character is festive and earthy, rooted in the folk music of Rajasthan's royal courts and desert communities. The raag uses both shuddha and komal Ni with considerable freedom, reflecting its folk origins where strict swara rules are secondary to emotional and contextual expression. Mand is particularly associated with the celebration of Gangaur festival and songs about love and separation in the desert landscape." },
  "Tilang": { swaras: "Sa Ga Ma Pa Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Ga Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga Ma Pa — Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga Sa — Ga Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", narrative: "Tilang is a light, pentatonic folk raag omitting Re and Dha, performed at night with a romantic, devotional character. It is closely associated with compositions praising Krishna and Radha, and its simple, direct phrase vocabulary makes it immediately emotive without complexity. The absence of Re and Dha gives the raag a stripped-down, devotional simplicity, and its characteristic Ga–Ma–Pa movement has an almost folk-song naturalness. Tilang is most at home in thumri and bhajan rather than extended khayal." },
  "Desh": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k)", vadi: "Pa", samvadi: "Re", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa — Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga Re Sa — Pa Ni(k) Sa — Dha Pa", narrative: "Desh (listed separately as a common variant name for the same raag) shares all characteristics with the Des entry above — its folk-patriotic warmth, its vakra arohana omitting Ga and Dha in ascent, and its intimate association with the monsoon season and the feeling of homeland. The spelling variant 'Desh' is commonly used interchangeably with 'Des' in different traditions and transcription systems." },
  "Mishra Khamaj": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k) Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga Ma Pa — Dha Pa Ma Ga — Ni(k) Dha Pa — Ni Sa", narrative: "Mishra Khamaj is Khamaj in its mixed or extended form, where additional swaras beyond the base Khamaj scale are employed for expressive effect. This mishra (mixed) approach is common in thumri, where strict raag boundaries are routinely crossed in service of emotional expression. The raag is highly versatile and is the standard vehicle for thumri performance in the Benares and Lucknow styles, allowing the singer maximum freedom to move through neighboring raags and return to the Khamaj home." },
  "Zila Kafi": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha Ni(k)", vadi: "Ma", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa — Dha Ni(k) Sa — Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Zila Kafi is a folk-festive raag closely related to Kafi but with a lighter, more celebratory character. It is particularly associated with the Holi festival and the spring season, and its folk origins give it a flexibility and informality unusual in classical contexts. The raag is performed in thumri and dadra styles and is rarely subjected to extended alap treatment, functioning instead as a vehicle for lively, rhythmically driven compositions." },
  "Bhairav": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Dha(k)", samvadi: "Re(k)", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga — Ma Ga Re(k) Sa — Ni(lower) Re(k) Ga Ma — Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", narrative: "Bhairav is one of the oldest and most revered raags in the entire Hindustani tradition, performed at dawn when the world hovers between night and day, darkness and light. Its komal Re and komal Dha give it an austere, solemn quality unlike any other — the music seems to come from a great depth, unhurried and deliberate. Named after Lord Shiva (Bhairava), the raag carries the weight of centuries of devotional practice, and its characteristic dwelling on komal Re and the andolan (oscillation) of komal Dha are among the most recognizable sounds in classical music. Bhairav is particularly associated with winter dawns, and its gravity and stillness are said to embody the moment of divine contact at the boundary of night. <em>Variance: The degree and speed of andolan on komal Dha varies considerably by gharana — the Agra and Gwalior gharanas use a slow, wide oscillation while the Jaipur gharana tends toward a narrower, more controlled one. The Kirana gharana, particularly through Ustad Abdul Karim Khan, is known for a deeply introverted, slow-unfolding treatment of Bhairav that differs markedly from the more assertive Gwalior approach. Some gharanas allow shuddha Ni in certain phrases while others strictly use only shuddha Ni as written.</em>" },
  "Ahir Bhairav": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k)", vadi: "Ma", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa — Dha Ni(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Ahir Bhairav is a morning raag that blends the gravity of Bhairav (komal Re) with the warmth of Kafi (komal Ni and shuddha Dha), creating a sound both austere and pastoral. The name references the Ahir community of herders, and the raag carries a folk-touched, outdoor quality — the feeling of early morning in the countryside rather than the temple. The combination of komal Re in the lower register and komal Ni near the top of the octave creates a beautiful arc of feeling, and the vadi Ma grounds the raag in a meditative middle register. It is one of the most widely loved morning raags." },
  "Nat Bhairav": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Pa", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga Ma Pa — Ni Dha Pa Ma — Ga Re(k) Sa — Pa Ni Sa", narrative: "Nat Bhairav combines the strength and energy of Nat with the gravity of Bhairav, using Bhairav's komal Re in a scale that otherwise ascends with considerable force. The result is a raag of strong contrasts — the softness of komal Re pulling against the directness of shuddha Ni and the bold Nat movements. It is performed in the morning and carries a quality of vigorous devotion — not the still meditation of Bhairav but active, energetic worship. Nat Bhairav is less common in concert performance but valued for its unusual character." },
  "Lalit": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Ma(t) Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ma(t)", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Ma(t) Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma — Ma(t) Dha(k) Ni Sa — Ni Dha(k) Ma(t) Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Lalit is one of the most mysterious and searching raags in the entire canon, performed in the pre-dawn hours when the mind is at its most inward. Its use of both Madhyams — shuddha and tivra — along with the complete absence of Pa creates a raag of profound harmonic strangeness: without Pancham, the usual sense of tonal anchor is removed, and the music floats in a way that is both unsettling and beautiful. The komal Re and komal Dha add further weight, and the characteristic interplay between the two Madhyams generates a searching, yearning quality. Lalit is considered one of the most difficult raags to perform convincingly and one of the most rewarding to hear." },
  "Ramkali": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga — Ma Ga Re(k) Sa — Ni(lower) Re(k) Ga Ma Pa — Dha(k) Ni Sa", narrative: "Ramkali is a dawn raag of ascetic stillness, closely related to Bhairav in its use of komal Re and komal Dha but with a softer, more meditative character. Where Bhairav has gravitas and weight, Ramkali has a gentler devotional quality — the difference between a stern saint and a peaceful one. The raag is associated with the dhrupad tradition and carries the unhurried, contemplative quality of that form. Its characteristic phrases move slowly and with deliberation, dwelling on each swara with care." },
  "Vibhas": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Pa Dha(k)", vadi: "Re(k)", samvadi: "Dha(k)", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Pa Dha(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Dha(k) Pa Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) — Pa Re(k) Sa — Dha(k) Pa Re(k) Sa — Sa Re(k) Pa", narrative: "Vibhas is a stark, austere dawn raag using only four swaras, making it among the sparsest raags in the tradition. The absence of Ga, Ma, and Ni removes almost all the usual melodic texture, leaving only bare bones: Sa, komal Re, Pa, and komal Dha. This extreme minimalism creates a raag of profound stillness — there is simply no room for ornamentation or melodic complexity, only the direct emotional impact of four swaras in their purest relationship. Vibhas is rarely heard in full concert performance and is considered more a meditative form than a performative one." },
  "Kalingada": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma — Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa — Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Kalingada is a rare dawn raag of deep melancholy, closely related to Bhairav but with its own phrase vocabulary and a more concentrated, introverted character. It is associated with the Kalinga region and carries a quality of ancient sorrow — not acute grief but the quiet, sustained melancholy of long memory. The raag is rarely performed in modern concerts and survives mainly in the repertoires of conservative dhrupad and khayal gharanas. When heard, its effect is immediate and striking." },
  "Jogiya": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ma Pa Dha(k)", vadi: "Re(k)", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Dha(k) Pa Ma Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ma — Pa Re(k) Sa — Dha(k) Pa Ma Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Jogiya is a dawn raag of ascetic wandering, evoking the jogi (yogi) who roams at the boundary of night and day. Its pentatonic scale with komal Re and komal Dha creates an eerie, untethered quality — appropriate for a raag about spiritual homelessness and the search that has no earthly destination. Omitting Ga and Ni removes two of the most emotionally expressive swaras, leaving a spare, almost naked sound. Jogiya is closely associated with winter dawns and is considered particularly atmospheric when performed in those conditions." },
  "Bhairav Bahar": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k)", vadi: "Ma", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa — Dha Ni(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Bhairav Bahar is a composite raag combining the austere dawn gravity of Bhairav (komal Re) with the spring exuberance of Bahar (komal Ni). The result is one of the most unusual raags in the tradition — the heaviness of Bhairav's komal Re coexisting with the light festivity of Bahar's character. It is associated with the morning of spring, where winter's chill has not yet fully lifted, and there is something both tender and paradoxical about the combination. The raag is rare but deeply beautiful when performed by a musician who understands how to balance its contrasting inheritances." },
  "Gurjari Todi": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma — Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa — Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Gurjari Todi is a morning raag from the Gujarat region, related to Todi but less ornate and more direct in its phrase vocabulary. It uses the same komal Re, komal Ga, and komal Dha as Todi but without the elaborate meends and gamaks that characterize the main Todi. The character is one of contained, dignified pathos — sorrowful but not overwrought — and the raag is considered more accessible than Miyan ki Todi while retaining its fundamental emotional character." },
  "Bairagi": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ma Pa Ni(k)", vadi: "Sa", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Pa Ma Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ma Pa — Ni(k) Pa Ma Re(k) Sa — Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", narrative: "Bairagi (also called Bairagi Bhairav) is a pentatonic dawn raag of deep spiritual detachment, named after the bairagi — the ascetic who has renounced worldly attachment. Its five swaras, including both komal Re (from Bhairav) and komal Ni, create a spare, otherworldly sound that seems to stand apart from both sorrow and joy. The raag conveys not grief but the profound peace of the one who has moved beyond ordinary emotional categories. It is a rare and highly atmospheric raag, most effective in the pre-dawn hours when the spiritual atmosphere of the music aligns with the atmosphere of the hour." },
  "Mishra Bhairav": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni Ni(k)", vadi: "Dha(k)", samvadi: "Re(k)", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa — Dha(k) Ni Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Mishra Bhairav is the mixed or lighter form of Bhairav, admitting additional swaras for expressive enrichment. It is more commonly performed in thumri and lighter khayal forms than the strict Bhairav, and its flexibility allows the vocalist to borrow phrases from neighboring raags while maintaining the Bhairav foundation through komal Re and komal Dha. The character is devotional but warmer and more approachable than pure Bhairav." },
  "Nata Bhairav": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Pa", samvadi: "Re(k)", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa — Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Nata Bhairav combines the bold, energetic phrases of Nat raag with the gravity of Bhairav's komal Re. The result is a morning raag of unusual vitality — the heavy pull of komal Re does not slow the music down but instead adds emotional depth to what would otherwise be a brisk, energetic raag. It is less commonly performed than Ahir Bhairav and Bhairav but occupies an important place in the Gwalior gharana repertoire." },
  "Lalita Gauri": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma — Dha(k) Ni Sa — Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa — Ma Ga Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Lalita Gauri is a rare dawn raag of gentle meditative quality, named after a form of the goddess Gauri. Its scale closely resembles Bhairav but with specific phrase movements that distinguish it. The character is peaceful and devotional, evoking the gentle aspect of the divine feminine in early morning light. The raag is rarely performed in mainstream concerts and survives primarily in the repertoires of conservative classical traditions." },
  "Yogi": { swaras: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Pa Ni(k)", vadi: "Sa", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Pa — Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Sa — Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", narrative: "Yogi is a pentatonic raag of ascetic stillness and slight eeriness, using komal Ga and komal Ni in a five-swara framework. The omission of Re and Dha, combined with both flat third and flat seventh, creates a sound simultaneously sparse and emotionally dense. The raag evokes the yogi in deep meditation — not the restless seeker but one who has achieved stillness. It carries a quality of mild Bhayanaka ras within its Shanta character, the peaceful fearlessness of one who has conquered ordinary emotion." },
  "Bhairavi": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma Pa — Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Bhairavi is the raag of farewell, universally performed as the concluding raag of any classical concert — a tradition so firmly established that a musician playing Bhairavi signals the end of the evening. All four komal swaras (Re, Ga, Dha, Ni) give it the most densely melancholic scale in the tradition, yet Bhairavi's emotional world is not simple sadness but the full complexity of departure: the bittersweet mix of gratitude, love, and the ache of ending. Importantly, Bhairavi is also a mishra raag by practice — additional swaras are routinely employed — and this gives it an unusual emotional breadth that allows it to encompass nearly any feeling within its farewell. It is perhaps the raag most deeply embedded in the collective emotional life of North India. <em>Variance: Bhairavi is the raag where gharana differences are most visible precisely because it is a mishra raag — the Patiala gharana is known for using shuddha Ma and shuddha Dha freely within Bhairavi, giving it a sweeter, brighter quality; the Gwalior tradition stays closer to the komal swaras. The Thumri styles of Benares and Lucknow each have distinct Bhairavi traditions with characteristic borrowed phrases. Whether to treat Bhairavi as a strict raag or a fluid mishra framework is itself a point of ongoing discussion between performers.</em>" },
  "Sindhu Bhairavi": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma Pa — Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re(k) Sa — Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k)", narrative: "Sindhu Bhairavi is a lighter, more romantic shade of Bhairavi, with the same scale but a phrase vocabulary that tends toward tender sweetness rather than deep pathos. Where Bhairavi is about farewell and the weight of ending, Sindhu Bhairavi is more about the gentle sadness of longing — its mood is that of a love song rather than a funeral hymn. It is widely performed in semi-classical and thumri formats and has been the basis for many well-known film songs." },
  "Malkauns": { swaras: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Ma", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Ma Ga(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga(k) Ma — Dha(k) Ma Ga(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Ma — Ga(k) Sa", narrative: "Malkauns (also called Malkosh) is one of the most powerful raags in the entire tradition, a pentatonic raag of the deep night that uses no Re or Pa — the two swaras most associated with movement and stability. This deliberate absence creates a raag of absolute stillness, its five swaras (Sa, komal Ga, Ma, komal Dha, komal Ni) forming a dark, densely chromatic cluster in the lower middle of the scale. The vadi Madhyam anchors everything, and the characteristic slow oscillations between the komal swaras have a hypnotic, meditative intensity. Legend holds that Malkauns was created by Shiva, and that its performance can summon supernatural forces — an indication of how deeply this raag has marked the imagination of listeners through the centuries. <em>Variance: The tempo and density of andolan on Ga(k) and Ni(k) varies significantly — the Agra gharana uses a pronounced, wide andolan on both while the Gwalior tradition moves through these swaras more directly. There is also gharana disagreement on whether the vadi is Ma or Sa: the Agra gharana strongly identifies Ma as vadi; some other traditions argue for Sa. A minority tradition also includes occasional use of komal Re as a grace note, which is rejected by most mainstream schools.</em>" },
  "Sampoorna Malkauns": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Ma", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa — Dha(k) Ma Ga(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma", narrative: "Sampoorna Malkauns is a seven-swara extension of Malkauns, adding Re and Pa to the pentatonic base. This addition softens the extreme density of pure Malkauns while retaining its dark, meditative character. The raag is less performed than pure Malkauns and is considered a somewhat academic variant, useful for exploring how the addition of the two absent swaras changes the raag's emotional character." },
  "Kafi": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa — Dha Ni(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Kafi is the parent thaat raag using komal Ga and komal Ni, and it carries a distinctly earthy, folk-rooted character that reflects the spring season and the outdoor world. The raag has deep connections to Holi music and the devotional poetry of the Bhakti movement, and its komal Ga has a tenderness and vulnerability at the core of its emotional identity. Kafi is unusual in being one of the few raags whose character feels simultaneously intimate and public — it is the raag of the village well as much as the concert stage — and this accessibility has made it a natural vehicle for folk-classical forms like thumri, dhrupad, and bhajan." },
  "Bageshri": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma — Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Bageshri is one of the most intimately beautiful raags in the late-night hours, a raag of feminine longing and deep emotional interiority. Its characteristic arohana skips Pa in ascent (moving directly from Ma to Dha), giving the raag an unusual melodic arc, and the three komal swaras — Ga, Dha, and Ni — create a dense, velvet quality in the middle register. The vadi komal Ga is the emotional heart of the raag, approached with slow, caressing meend from Re or Ma. Bageshri is classically described as a young woman waiting for her beloved late at night — its mood is not desperate longing but the patient, deeply felt anticipation of love. <em>Variance: Whether Pa appears in arohana is the most significant gharana distinction — the Kirana gharana strictly omits Pa in ascent; the Gwalior and some Patiala traditions allow a brief Pa touch in ascending phrases. The Jaipur gharana additionally uses a characteristic phrase that touches Dha before descending, which is avoided in stricter traditions. The vadi is universally Ga(k), but the degree to which Dha(k) is dwelt upon varies considerably.</em>" },
  "Bhimpalasi": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa — Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) — Re Sa Ni(lower) Re Ga(k) Ma", narrative: "Bhimpalasi is the quintessential afternoon raag of tender longing, combining Kafi thaat (komal Ga and Ni) with the shuddha Dha and a vakra arohana that skips Dha in ascent. This omission of Dha in the rise gives the raag a reaching, open quality, as though the music is stretching toward something slightly out of reach. The vadi komal Gandhar is approached with characteristic meend and is the site of the raag's greatest expressiveness. Performed in the afternoon when the day's business has quieted and the mind becomes more emotionally receptive, Bhimpalasi occupies a unique emotional niche — it is the raag of wistful afternoon awareness, the feeling of beauty tinged with the knowledge that it cannot last." },
  "Dhani": { swaras: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Pa Ni(k)", vadi: "Pa", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Pa — Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Sa — Ga(k) Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", narrative: "Dhani is a pentatonic night raag of gentle intimacy, omitting Re and Dha and using komal Ga and komal Ni. The absence of Re removes the second degree — a swara that often provides opening and direction — creating a raag that circles inward rather than reaching outward. The characteristic Pa–Ni–Sa movement is warm and direct, and the overall character is one of quiet, personal expressiveness. Dhani is closely related to Charukeshi in some respects but has its own phrase vocabulary and a distinctly more intimate, less formally structured character." },
  "Patdeep": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Ni(k)", vadi: "Pa", samvadi: "Re", arohana: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa — Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa — Pa Ni(k) Sa", narrative: "Patdeep is an afternoon raag of quiet contemplation, closely related to Bhimpalasi but with shuddha Ni rather than komal Ni in some movements — a point of gharana variation. Its character is more understated than Bhimpalasi, less urgently longing and more calmly reflective. Performed in the afternoon hours, it embodies the quality of afternoon stillness — the inward turn of a mind at rest, neither asleep nor actively engaged, simply present. The raag is particularly associated with slow, meditative khayal compositions." },
  "Chandrakauns": { swaras: "Ga(k) Ma Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Ma Ga(k) Sa", aalap: "Ga(k) Ma Dha(k) — Ni(k) Dha(k) Ma Ga(k) — Sa Ga(k) Ma Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", narrative: "Chandrakauns is one of the most hypnotic and ethereal raags in the tradition, a late-night raag that omits Re and Pa entirely and uses only four swaras (Sa, komal Ga, Ma, komal Dha, komal Ni — with Sa used sparingly as a touch note in some traditions). The absence of Pa — the most stable and grounding of all swaras — gives the raag a floating, unanchored quality, as though the music exists in a state of perpetual suspension. The three consecutive komal swaras (Ga, Dha, Ni) create a dense chromatic cluster that sounds at once ancient and otherworldly. Chandrakauns is named after the moon (Chandra) and carries a lunar, silvery quality appropriate to the late night hours." },
  "Megh": { swaras: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni(k)", vadi: "Ma", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Pa Ma Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa — Ni(k) Pa Ma Re Sa — Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", narrative: "Megh (meaning cloud) is the foundational monsoon raag, performed to invoke and celebrate the arrival of rain. Its pentatonic scale omits Ga and Dha, creating a raag of wide, open intervals that somehow evoke the expansiveness of the sky filling with clouds. The characteristic phrases move in a rolling, cumulative way — like the building of a monsoon storm — and the komal Ni adds a touch of longing appropriate to the anticipation of rain after summer's heat. Megh is one of the earliest raags documented in classical treatises and its association with rain is among the oldest in the tradition." },
  "Miyan ki Malhar": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Ni(k) Ni", vadi: "Ni", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa — Ni(k) Ni(k) Sa — Ni Ni(k) Pa Ma — Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Miyan ki Malhar, attributed to the legendary Tansen of Akbar's court, is considered the supreme raag of the monsoon and one of the most emotionally profound raags in the entire tradition. Its use of both shuddha and komal Ni creates characteristic vacillating movements between the two — particularly the signature Ni-Ni(k) oscillation that captures the sound of rain itself. The raag has a quality of cosmic pathos — not personal sorrow but the vast, impersonal melancholy of the monsoon, the dissolution of summer's heat and dryness into the relief and sorrow of rain. Legend holds that Tansen sang this raag and caused rain to fall, and its power in performance remains undiminished across centuries. <em>Variance: The arohana is one of the most debated aspects — the Gwalior tradition uses a strict vakra ascending scale skipping Ga; the Agra gharana allows brief touches of Ga in ascent. The Ni–Ni(k) oscillation speed and extent varies: the Agra style uses a slow, wide meend between the two Ni variants while the Jaipur style is more contained. The vadi is identified as Ni in the standard reading, but some older sources give Pa as the vadi.</em>" },
  "Gaud Malhar": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha Ni(k) Ni", vadi: "Pa", samvadi: "Re", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa — Ni Dha Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Gaud Malhar blends the qualities of Gaud (brightness, romance) with the Malhar family's monsoon character, creating a raag of romantic rain-soaked sweetness. It is lighter in emotional weight than Miyan ki Malhar, more joyful and less mournful — the feeling of dancing in the first rain rather than the deep contemplation of the storm. The raag uses both Ni variants and has characteristic phrases that blend the openness of Gaud with the characteristic meends of the Malhar family." },
  "Nat Malhar": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni(k) Ni", vadi: "Pa", samvadi: "Re", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa — Ni Sa — Ni(k) Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", narrative: "Nat Malhar combines the energetic boldness of Nat with the monsoon character of the Malhar family. Where Miyan ki Malhar is introspective and profound, Nat Malhar is outward and exuberant — the celebration of rain rather than its contemplation. The Nat elements bring strong, direct melodic phrases and the Malhar elements bring the characteristic Ni oscillations and the sense of rain. Nat Malhar is among the most energetic of the monsoon raags." },
  "Sur Malhar": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Re", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa — Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Sur Malhar is the most meditative and introverted of the principal Malhar raags, using komal Dha alongside the komal Ga and Ni to create a denser, more inward sound than the other Malhars. Where Miyan ki Malhar is profound and Nat Malhar is exuberant, Sur Malhar is quiet and contemplative — the rain falling in stillness rather than in dramatic storms. The raag is associated with deep introspection and is considered one of the more difficult Malhars to perform convincingly due to its understated character." },
  "Shuddha Sarang": { swaras: "Sa Re Ma Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Re", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa — Ni Pa Ma Re Sa — Re Ma Pa Ni Sa", narrative: "Shuddha Sarang is the purest form of the Sarang family, a summer afternoon raag of calm clarity. Its characteristic vakra arohana — skipping Ga and going directly from Re to Ma — gives it an open, forward-reaching quality, and the absence of Ga throughout gives it a somewhat sparse but pure sound. The Sarang family is associated with the afternoon heat of summer, and Shuddha Sarang embodies this through its direct, unadorned beauty. The raag is an important teaching raag and is considered foundational for understanding the Sarang family." },
  "Vrindavani Sarang": { swaras: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni", vadi: "Re", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Pa Ma Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni Sa — Ni Pa Ma Re Sa — Re Ma Pa Ni Sa", narrative: "Vrindavani Sarang evokes the forest of Vrindavan where Krishna played his flute in the summer afternoons, and its character is playful, pastoral, and touched with the longing of that mythological world. It is a pentatonic raag (no Ga or Dha) whose five clear swaras create a sound of beautiful simplicity. Performed in the afternoon heat of summer, the raag captures perfectly the combination of heat, stillness, and the bittersweet longing for relief — whether that relief is rain, nightfall, or the sound of Krishna's flute across the forest." },
  "Madhyamad Sarang": { swaras: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni", vadi: "Ma", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Pa Ma Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Ma Pa — Ni Pa Ma Re Sa — Ma Pa Ni Sa", narrative: "Madhyamad Sarang is a summer afternoon raag from the Sarang family whose vadi is Ma rather than Re, giving it a slightly more grounded, meditative quality compared to other Sarangs. Its pentatonic scale (Sa Re Ma Pa Ni) is the same as Vrindavani Sarang but the emphasis on Ma creates a different emotional center. The raag is associated with the stillness of midday heat and has a contemplative summer-afternoon quality." },
  "Brindavani Sarang": { swaras: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni", vadi: "Re", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Pa Ma Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni Sa — Ni Pa Ma Re Sa — Re Ma Pa Ni Sa", narrative: "Brindavani Sarang (a common alternate spelling of Vrindavani Sarang) shares the same characteristics as its counterpart — the Vrindavan imagery, the summer afternoon context, and the pentatonic brightness. In some gharanas the two names refer to the same raag, while in others slight differences in phrase emphasis and treatment distinguish them. Either way, the raag's essential character — pastoral festivity and the longing of the summer afternoon — remains constant." },
  "Gaur Sarang": { swaras: "Sa Re Ma Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Re", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa Dha — Ni Dha Pa Ma Re Sa — Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa", narrative: "Gaur Sarang blends the Sarang family characteristics with elements of Gaur raag, adding Dha to the pentatonic Sarang base and giving the raag a slightly more complete, warmer sound. The addition of Dhaivata rounds out the phrases and adds brightness and expansiveness that the pure Sarangs lack. It is a summer afternoon raag of gentle beauty, its character sitting between the stark simplicity of the pure Sarangs and the fuller emotional world of raags with all seven swaras." },
  "Bahar": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha — Ni(k) Sa — Dha Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Bahar is the quintessential spring raag, associated with the season of flowering and renewal. Its scale from Kafi thaat (komal Ga and Ni) gives it an earthy, folk-rooted warmth, and its characteristic vakra arohana skipping Re in ascent gives it a forward-leaping, exuberant quality. Bahar is most at home in festive compositions celebrating spring, Holi, and the return of warmth, and its joyful, folk-tinged character has made it one of the most beloved seasonal raags. It is often paired or compared with Basant, the other great spring raag." },
  "Pilu": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha — Ni(k) Dha Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Pilu is a gentle, folksy raag that defies strict classification — it is one of the most 'mishra' (mixed) raags in the tradition, where the rules are relaxed and swaras from neighboring raags appear freely in service of emotional expression. It is above all a thumri raag, and in the hands of great thumri singers it becomes a fluid, intimate vehicle for the direct expression of longing and love. Pilu is performed at any time and in any season, which itself reflects its fluid, unclassifiable nature. Its character is warm, direct, and deeply human." },
  "Nayaki Kanada": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa — Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Nayaki Kanada is a late-night raag of tender, feminine character from the Kanada family. It uses three komal swaras (Ga, Dha, Ni) to create a soft, intimate sound, and its characteristic phrase movements are gentle and inward-turning. The raag is associated with the nayika (heroine) of classical poetry — a young woman in an emotionally charged situation — and its intimate, personal quality makes it a natural vehicle for compositions about love and longing." },
  "Jog": { swaras: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Pa Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga(k) Ma Pa — Ni(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Sa — Ga(k) Ma Pa Ni(k) Sa", narrative: "Jog is a mysterious, atmospheric night raag that combines elements of Bhairavi and Kafi to create a pentatonic scale of unusual beauty. Omitting Re and Dha, it uses komal Ga and komal Ni alongside Sa, Ma, and Pa. The result is a raag of floating, dreamlike quality — neither firmly grounded in any single emotional world but moving between contemplation and gentle longing. Jog is popular in semi-classical and film music contexts and is considered one of the more accessible of the pentatonic night raags, its five swaras falling naturally under the fingers and voice." },
  "Asavari": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Dha(k)", samvadi: "Ga(k)", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Dha(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa Dha(k) — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Asavari is a morning raag of austere, detached gravity, the parent thaat raag for its family. Its characteristic vakra arohana — skipping Ga and Ni in ascent — gives it a sparse, direct quality, and the three komal swaras (Ga, Dha, Ni) in descent create a deeply melancholic, introspective sound. The vadi komal Dhaivata sits in the upper register and is the point of greatest expressiveness. Asavari is an ancient raag with roots stretching back to early Sanskrit musical treatises, and its restrained, dignified sorrow is considered one of the purest expressions of Karuna ras." },
  "Jaunpuri": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Dha(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa — Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa", narrative: "Jaunpuri is a morning raag of sober, understated sadness, sharing much of its scale with Asavari but distinguished through specific phrase movements and emotional character. Named after the city of Jaunpur, it carries a quality of dignified, contained grief — not the outpouring of Bhairavi but the quiet, continuing sadness of long acquaintance with sorrow. The raag is performed in the late morning and is considered one of the more austere of the morning raags, its characteristic phrases moving with measured deliberateness." },
  "Darbari Kanada": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Re", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re — Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) — Re Sa", narrative: "Darbari Kanada is one of the most majestic and gravity-laden raags in the Hindustani tradition, performed deep in the night in a slow, deliberate tempo that brooks no hurry. Associated with the court of Emperor Akbar and attributed to Tansen himself, its character is that of the durbar (royal court) — formal, magnificent, and deeply serious. The characteristic andolan (slow oscillation) on komal Ga and komal Ni gives the raag its distinctive sound, a heavy, deliberate wavering that seems to carry the weight of history. The vadi Re in the lower register and the characteristic long dwelling on each swara create a raag of profound unhurriedness — Darbari cannot be rushed without destroying its essence. <em>Variance: The andolan on Ga(k) and Ni(k) is the most debated element across gharanas — the Agra gharana uses a particularly slow, wide andolan on both swaras, while the Gwalior tradition uses a somewhat quicker oscillation. The Jaipur gharana omits or minimizes the andolan on Ni(k) altogether. The arohana treatment also varies: some traditions include a characteristic Ga(k) in the ascent while others strictly avoid it. The vadi is identified as Re in most traditions, but some Kanada-family treatises assign it to Ma.</em>" },
  "Adana": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Pa", samvadi: "Re", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa Ni Sa — Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Adana is a late-night raag of considerable energy and assertiveness, distinguishing itself from its Asavari cousins through the use of shuddha Ni rather than komal Ni in ascent. This shuddha Ni gives the arohana a bold, reaching quality, and the raag has a vigorous, confident character quite different from the melancholic introspection of Darbari. Adana is particularly associated with compositions of heroic or passionate character and is considered one of the most energetic of the late-night raags." },
  "Komal Rishabh Asavari": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Dha(k)", samvadi: "Ga(k)", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ma Pa — Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Komal Rishabh Asavari is a variant of Asavari that adds komal Re to the already komal Ga, Dha, and Ni, creating an even denser chromatic texture. The addition of komal Re deepens the melancholic character considerably — all four possible komal swaras are present, creating a scale that is almost entirely in the minor and double-minor territory. It is a rare and deeply melancholic raag, considered more austere than standard Asavari." },
  "Gandhari": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Re Ma Pa Dha(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ma Pa — Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Gandhari is a rare morning raag of dark, grieving character, named after the blind queen of the Mahabharata who was a figure of monumental, stoic sorrow. Its scale is closely related to Asavari but its phrase vocabulary and emotional character are distinctly its own — heavier, more concentrated in its grief, with characteristic phrases that seem to circle the same wound repeatedly. The raag is rarely performed in mainstream concerts and survives in the repertoires of conservative gharanas." },
  "Kirwani": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa — Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma Ga Re Sa", narrative: "Kirwani is a hauntingly beautiful night raag of Carnatic origin that has been fully absorbed into Hindustani practice. Its scale — with shuddha Re and Ga but komal Dha and Ni — closely resembles the Western harmonic minor scale, which gives it an immediately striking, somewhat exotic quality to ears trained in either tradition. The contrast between the brightness of shuddha Ga and the shadow of komal Dha creates a raag of beautiful ambiguity — romantic and slightly melancholic at once. Kirwani is relatively modern in the Hindustani repertoire but has become extremely popular due to its immediately appealing, distinctive sound." },
  "Miyan ki Todi": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Dha(k)", samvadi: "Ga(k)", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) — Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa — Ni(lower) Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) — Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", narrative: "Miyan ki Todi, simply called Todi, is considered by many musicians to be the most complete and profound raag in the entire Hindustani canon — the raag that demands the most of a performer and offers the most to a listener. Its scale combines komal Re, komal Ga, tivra Ma, and komal Dha — an extraordinary concentration of altered swaras that creates a sound of intense, concentrated pathos. Attributed to Tansen, it is performed in the mid-morning and requires a slow, deliberate unfolding; every meend, every oscillation carries weight. The characteristic andolan on komal Ga and the way the tivra Ma pulls away from the komal Ga below it creates a tension that is the musical embodiment of the raag's emotional world: exquisite, irreducible suffering. <em>Variance: The vadi is universally accepted as Dha(k) today, but older treatises sometimes list Ga(k). The treatment of Pa is a significant point of gharana difference — the Gwalior and Agra traditions use Pa sparingly and fleetingly, almost as a passing tone, while the Jaipur gharana gives it more sustained emphasis. The Kirana gharana is associated with an extremely slow, deeply ornamented approach to Todi where each meend is elaborated at length; the Gwalior approach tends to be more structurally direct.</em>" },
  "Multani": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ma(t)", samvadi: "Sa", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa — Dha(k) Ni Sa — Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Multani is Todi's afternoon counterpart, sharing the same scale but performed at a different hour and with a different emotional character. Where Todi is all intensity and concentrated pathos, Multani is heavier, more somber, and more meditative — the weight of Todi's emotion experienced through the lens of afternoon stillness. The vadi tivra Ma (rather than Todi's komal Dha) grounds the raag in the middle register, giving it a more contained, inward quality. Multani is considered one of the most demanding raags to perform in the afternoon concert slot." },
  "Bilaskhani Todi": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni(k)", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni(k)", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) — Pa Dha(k) Ni(k) Sa — Ni(k) Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Bilaskhani Todi is attributed to Bilas Khan, son of the great Tansen, and legend holds that it was created at his father's deathbed — giving it an association with mourning and grief that goes beyond any other raag. Its scale differs from Miyan ki Todi in using komal Ni rather than shuddha Ni, which adds an additional layer of melancholic density. The raag is said to be so mournful that it can revive the dead, and its character is among the most deeply sorrowful in the tradition. It is rarely performed and considered among the most emotionally demanding raags to sing." },
  "Gujari Todi": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa — Dha(k) Ni Sa — Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Gujari Todi is a morning raag from the Gujarat region, related to the Todi family but with a softer, less intense emotional character. Its phrase vocabulary is less ornate than Miyan ki Todi, and the overall character is one of dignified, contained sadness rather than acute pathos. The raag is valued for its accessibility relative to the demanding Todi standard while retaining the essential emotional gravity of the Todi family." },
  "Madhuvanti": { swaras: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma(t) Ga(k) Re Sa", aalap: "Sa Re Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa — Dha Ni Sa — Ni Dha Pa Ma(t) Ga(k) Re Sa", narrative: "Madhuvanti is a relatively modern raag — widely attributed to 20th-century musicians — that has become enormously popular due to its immediately beautiful, bittersweet character. It blends the komal Ga of Kafi with the tivra Ma of Kalyan, creating an unusual combination that feels simultaneously bright and shadowed — sweet pathos rather than heavy sorrow. The raag has been called 'sweet' (madhu = honey) for good reason: its emotional world is that of gentle, warm melancholy rather than grief. It is performed in the afternoon and has been used extensively in semi-classical and film music contexts." },
  "Shuddha Todi": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Dha(k)", samvadi: "Ga(k)", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) — Dha(k) Ni Sa — Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa — Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Shuddha Todi is the strict, orthodox form of Todi using all the canonical alterations without any vakra movements or borrowed swaras. It is considered the purest expression of the Todi emotional world — concentrated, intense pathos without any of the ornamental complexity that characterizes Miyan ki Todi's characteristic meends and gamaks. In practice, the term often refers to the same raag as Miyan ki Todi but emphasizes a more direct, unornamented approach to the scale." },
  "Bhupali Todi": { swaras: "Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) — Dha(k) Ni Sa — Ni Dha(k) Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Bhupali Todi is an unusual pentatonic variant of Todi, omitting Pa and using only five swaras from the Todi scale. The absence of Pa — the most grounding swara — creates an even more floating, unanchored quality than standard Todi, which is already unsettled. It is a rare and highly specialized raag, primarily of theoretical and academic interest, though in skilled hands its concentrated use of altered swaras can be deeply moving." },
  "Gujri Todi": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ga(k)", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga(k) Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) — Ni Sa — Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga(k) Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Gujri Todi (an alternate name for Gujari Todi) is the Gujarati regional variant of Todi, characterized by gentler phrase movements and a somewhat more approachable emotional character compared to Miyan ki Todi. The raag represents the regional diversity within the Todi family and shows how a single emotional world can be expressed with different degrees of intensity and ornamentation." },
  "Purvi": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa — Dha(k) Ni Sa — Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Purvi is a twilight raag of deep, austere contemplation, performed at the meeting of day and night when the sun is setting. Its combination of komal Re, shuddha Ga, tivra Ma, and komal Dha creates a scale of unusual tension and gravity — the tivra Ma pulling upward while the komal swaras on either side pull down. The raag has no easy emotional resolution; it sits in a state of permanent yearning at the threshold between two worlds. This liminal quality makes Purvi one of the most psychologically intense raags, and it demands a level of stillness and concentration from both performer and listener that few other raags require." },
  "Shree": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ma(t) Pa Ni", vadi: "Re(k)", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ma(t) Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Pa Ma(t) Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ma(t) Pa — Ni Pa Ma(t) Re(k) Sa — Ma(t) Pa Ni Sa", narrative: "Shree is a twilight raag of stark, urgent devotion, one of the most austere raags in the tradition. A pentatonic raag omitting Ga and Dha, using komal Re and tivra Ma, its bare five swaras carry an almost elemental quality — stripped of all ornamentation, what remains is pure, concentrated emotion. The raag is associated with the atmosphere of the setting sun and carries a quality of urgent supplication, as though the fading light is itself a prayer. Shree is one of the oldest raags in the North Indian tradition and appears in the earliest raag classifications. Its intensity is such that it is considered one of the most demanding raags for both performer and listener." },
  "Puriya Dhanashri": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa — Dha(k) Ni Sa — Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Puriya Dhanashri is a richly complex twilight raag that blends elements of Puriya (from the Marwa thaat) and Dhanashri, creating one of the most emotionally multilayered raags in the tradition. Its combination of komal Re, shuddha Ga, tivra Ma, and komal Dha creates a scale of beautiful tension, and its phrase vocabulary draws on both parent raags to create something more complete and emotionally nuanced than either alone. The vadi Gandhar is warm and expressive, and the characteristic movements through the komal swaras add depth and shadow to the basic romantic character. Puriya Dhanashri is considered one of the great sunset raags." },
  "Basant": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Ga Ma(t) Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Ga Ma(t) Pa Ni Sa — Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Basant is the great spring night raag, a raag of festive joy and renewal associated with the Basant Panchami festival and the flowering of spring. Its vakra arohana skipping Re in ascent gives it a forward-leaping, exuberant quality, and its combination of Purvi thaat swaras with a spring-celebrating character creates an unusual raag: the solemnity of the scale in tension with the festivity of the season. Basant is traditionally performed in the night during spring and is associated with compositions in the braj bhasha (Old Hindi) tradition celebrating the colors and abundance of spring." },
  "Paraj": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni", vadi: "Ni", samvadi: "Ga", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha(k) Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa — Dha(k) Ni Sa — Ni Dha(k) Pa Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Paraj is a rare late-night raag of profound inwardness from the Purvi thaat. Its scale is nearly identical to Purvi but its vadi, samvadi, and phrase vocabulary create an entirely different emotional world — quieter, more personal, more introspective. The raag is considered one of the most difficult to perform convincingly precisely because its character is so understated; without careful attention to the specific phrase shapes, it collapses into Purvi or Puriya Dhanashri. Paraj is a raag for the deeply initiated." },
  "Marwa": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Dha Ni", vadi: "Re(k)", samvadi: "Dha", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) — Dha Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa — Ni Dha Ma(t) Ga", narrative: "Marwa is one of the most psychologically unsettling raags in the Hindustani tradition — deliberately so. The complete absence of Pa, the most grounding and stabilizing of all swaras, creates a raag without any tonal anchor. Combined with komal Re and tivra Ma, the scale contains extreme tensions that never resolve: the music reaches upward without ever finding a stable resting point. Performed at sunset, Marwa captures the restlessness and incompleteness of twilight — neither day nor night, neither resolution nor release. The vadi komal Re, sitting at the very foundation of the scale, is the primary source of the raag's distinctive tension." },
  "Puriya": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Dha Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Ni(lower) Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Ni(lower) Re(k) Ga Ma(t) — Dha Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa — Ni Dha Ma(t) Ga", narrative: "Puriya is a mysterious, deeply yearning sunset raag closely related to Marwa (both omit Pa and use komal Re and tivra Ma) but with its own distinctive phrase vocabulary and emotional character. Where Marwa is restless and unresolved, Puriya is more melancholic and searching — a raag of profound longing rather than existential restlessness. Its characteristic arohana begins from the lower Ni, creating a phrase that seems to emerge from depth and reach upward, and the vadi Gandhar is the site of its greatest emotional intensity." },
  "Sohini": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Pa Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa — Ni Pa Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa — Ga Ma(t) Pa Ni Sa", narrative: "Sohini is a night raag of romantic longing and melancholic beauty, using komal Re and tivra Ma to create a scale of beautiful tension. Unlike Marwa and Puriya which omit Pa, Sohini retains it — which grounds the raag somewhat and prevents it from becoming wholly unresolved. The characteristic movements through komal Re and the way the tivra Ma resolves to Pa give the raag its particular sound: reaching and longing but with occasional moments of sweet resolution. Sohini is associated with the folk story of Sohini, who swam nightly to meet her beloved Mahiwal, and its romantic character is appropriate to this legend." },
  "Bhatiyar": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni", vadi: "Ga", samvadi: "Ni", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa Dha Ni Sa", avarohana: "Sa Ni Dha Pa Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) Ga Ma(t) Pa — Dha Ni Sa — Ni Dha Pa Ma(t) Ga Re(k) Sa", narrative: "Bhatiyar is a rare and atmospheric dawn raag of profound gravity, one of the most rarely heard raags in regular concert performance. It combines elements of the Marwa family (komal Re, tivra Ma) with Dha, and its character is one of ancient, weighty contemplation appropriate to the pre-dawn hours. The raag is associated with the atmosphere of deep winter mornings — the darkness before dawn when the world is at its most still and the mind at its most inward. Its rarity in performance has given it an almost legendary quality among connoisseurs." },
  "Bibhas": { swaras: "Sa Re(k) Pa Dha(k)", vadi: "Re(k)", samvadi: "Pa", arohana: "Sa Re(k) Pa Dha(k) Sa", avarohana: "Sa Dha(k) Pa Re(k) Sa", aalap: "Sa Re(k) — Pa Re(k) Sa — Dha(k) Pa Re(k) Sa — Re(k) Pa Dha(k) Sa", narrative: "Bibhas is an extremely sparse dawn raag using only four swaras — Sa, komal Re, Pa, and komal Dha — creating one of the most minimalist raags in the tradition. This extreme austerity is intentional: the bare bones of the scale correspond to the bare bones of dawn consciousness, the mind not yet fully awake, the world not yet fully present. It is a raag of pure atmosphere rather than melodic development, and its character is one of profound, winter-morning stillness. Bibhas is rarely performed in full concert settings and is considered more a meditative or atmospheric form." }
};
